---
import StandardPageLayout from "../../layouts/StandardPageLayout.astro";
import FinancialStatementKey from "../../components/FinancialStatementKey.astro";
import Glossary from "../../components/Glossary.astro";
import PageToc from "../../components/PageToc.astro";
import "../../styles/standards-poster.css";
import "../../styles/extended.css";
import HistoryTimeline from "../../components/HistoryTimeline.astro";
import history from "../../data/history.json";
import metadata from "../../data/standards/ifrs/ifrs8.json";
---

<StandardPageLayout
  code={metadata.code}
  standardType={metadata.framework}
  title={metadata.title}
  description={metadata.description}
  issued={metadata.issued}
  latestAmendment={metadata.latestAmendment}
  status={metadata.status}
  summary={metadata.summary}
>
  <Fragment slot="toc">
    <PageToc
      items={[
        { id: "s1", label: "Conceptual Framework Linkage" },
        { id: "s2", label: "Scope & Applicability" },
        { id: "s3", label: "Identifying Operating Segments" },
        { id: "s4", label: "Aggregation of Segments" },
        { id: "s5", label: "The Quantitative Thresholds — 10% Tests" },
        { id: "s6", label: "The 75% Reporting Sufficiency Test" },
        { id: "s7", label: "Measurement of Segment Information" },
        { id: "s8", label: "Reconciliations to Consolidated Totals" },
        { id: "s9", label: "Entity-Wide Disclosures" },
        { id: "s10", label: "Critical Judgments & Estimates" },
        { id: "s11", label: "IFRS vs US GAAP" },
        { id: "s12", label: "Statement Mapping" },
        { id: "s13", label: "Related Standards" },
        { id: "s14", label: "Glossary" },
        { id: "history", label: "History & Development" },
      ]}
    />
  </Fragment>
  <div class="wrap">

    <!-- ============ SECTION 1 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s1"><span class="num">1</span><h2>Conceptual Framework Linkage</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci">
        <div class="ctitle ci">Why segment reporting uses "the management approach"</div>
        <div class="principle">The Conceptual Framework values information that helps users understand a business the way MANAGEMENT itself does. IFRS 8 adopts this "MANAGEMENT APPROACH" directly: segments are defined by how the entity is ACTUALLY organised and managed internally, not by an externally imposed classification scheme.</div>
        <div class="elabel">HOW IFRS 8 MAPS TO THE FRAMEWORK</div>
        <div class="check-list">
          <span><strong>Relevance</strong> — reporting segments the way management ACTUALLY monitors them gives users the SAME lens management uses to run the business</span>
          <span><strong>Faithful representation</strong> — externally imposed, standardised segment categories risk distorting how the business is genuinely structured and managed</span>
          <span><strong>Cost constraint</strong> — segment information is DERIVED from data management ALREADY produces internally</span>
        </div>
        <div class="note">IFRS 8 replaced IAS 14's earlier "business/geographical segment" model specifically to align segment reporting more closely with how entities are ACTUALLY managed.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="card cs">
        <div class="ctitle cs">Measurement principles applied by IFRS 8</div>
        <div class="principle">Unlike most IFRS Standards, IFRS 8 does NOT prescribe how segment items are measured — it requires reporting the SAME measures management ITSELF uses internally, even if those measures differ from IFRS.</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th cols-13-1"><span>Framework concept</span><span>IFRS 8's application</span></div>
          <div class="tr cols-13-1"><span>Measurement basis</span><span>WHATEVER basis management uses internally — may differ from IFRS</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt cols-13-1"><span>Reconciliation requirement</span><span>Mandatory reconciliation of segment totals to consolidated IFRS totals</span></div>
        </div>
        <div class="callout info callout--flush">ℹ️ Permitting NON-IFRS internal measures, but requiring a mandatory reconciliation, balances <strong>relevance</strong> against <strong>comparability</strong>.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 2 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s2"><span class="num">2</span><h2>Scope &amp; Applicability</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cw">
        <div class="ctitle cw">What IFRS 8 covers</div>
        <div class="principle">IFRS 8 applies to the SEPARATE or INDIVIDUAL financial statements of an entity, and the CONSOLIDATED financial statements of a group with a parent, whose DEBT or EQUITY instruments are traded in a public market (or that FILES its financial statements with a regulatory organisation for the purpose of issuing such instruments).</div>
        <div class="note">Unlike most IFRS Standards, IFRS 8's scope is EXPLICITLY tied to PUBLIC ACCOUNTABILITY — private entities without publicly traded instruments are generally NOT required to apply IFRS 8.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="card cd">
        <div class="ctitle cd">Key definitions you must know</div>
        <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Operating segment</div><div class="glossary-def">A component engaging in business activities generating revenues/expenses, whose results are REGULARLY REVIEWED by the CODM, with DISCRETE financial information available.</div></div>
        <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Chief operating decision maker (CODM)</div><div class="glossary-def">A FUNCTION, not necessarily a job title — allocates resources to, and assesses performance of, operating segments.</div></div>
        <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Reportable segment</div><div class="glossary-def">An operating segment (or aggregation) meeting one of the quantitative thresholds, or voluntarily reported.</div></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 3 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s3"><span class="num">3</span><h2>Identifying Operating Segments (with worked example)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci span2">
        <div class="ctitle ci">The three-part definition, applied</div>
        <div class="principle">A component is an operating segment ONLY if it meets ALL THREE conditions: (1) engages in business activities generating revenue/expenses; (2) results REGULARLY reviewed by the CODM; and (3) DISCRETE financial information is available.</div>
        <div class="worked worked--flush">
          <div class="wk-title">Example 1: Identifying operating segments via internal reporting</div>
          <div class="wk-body">A diversified group's CEO (the CODM) receives monthly reports broken into three divisions: Electronics, Textiles, and Logistics — each with discrete revenue/expense/profit figures. A fourth cost centre, "Corporate HQ," generates no revenue and is not reviewed for resource allocation.</div>
          <div class="wk-answer">Electronics, Textiles, and Logistics EACH qualify as operating segments. Corporate HQ does NOT (no revenue-generating activity, not assessed for resource allocation).</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 4 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s4"><span class="num">4</span><h2>Aggregation of Segments</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cs span2">
        <div class="ctitle cs">Combining similar segments — a strict five-factor test</div>
        <div class="principle">Two or more operating segments MAY be aggregated if consistent with IFRS 8's core principles, with SIMILAR ECONOMIC CHARACTERISTICS, and similar in EACH respect:</div>
        <div class="check-list">
          <span>Nature of products and services</span>
          <span>Nature of the production processes</span>
          <span>Type or class of customer</span>
          <span>Methods used to distribute products/services</span>
          <span>Nature of the regulatory environment, if applicable</span>
        </div>
        <div class="note">Aggregation is genuine JUDGEMENT, not mechanical calculation.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 5 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s5"><span class="num">5</span><h2>The Quantitative Thresholds — 10% Tests (with worked example)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2">
        <div class="ctitle cd">Reportable if it meets ANY ONE of three 10% tests</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th cols-1-16"><span>Test</span><span>Threshold</span></div>
          <div class="tr cols-1-16"><span>Revenue test</span><span>Reported revenue (external + intersegment) is 10%+ of COMBINED revenue of all segments</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt cols-1-16"><span>Profit or loss test</span><span>Absolute profit/loss is 10%+ of the GREATER of combined profit of profit-making segments, combined loss of loss-making segments</span></div>
          <div class="tr cols-1-16"><span>Assets test</span><span>Segment assets are 10%+ of combined assets of all segments</span></div>
        </div>
        <div class="worked worked--flush">
          <div class="wk-title">Example 2: Applying the profit/loss 10% test</div>
          <div class="wk-body">4 segments: A ($800k profit), B ($600k profit) → combined profits $1,400,000. C ($300k loss), D ($900k loss) → combined losses $1,200,000.</div>
          <div class="wk-answer">Benchmark = GREATER of $1,400,000 and $1,200,000 = <strong>$1,400,000</strong>. 10% threshold = $140,000. Segment D's $900,000 loss exceeds this → REPORTABLE.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 6 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s6"><span class="num">6</span><h2>The 75% Reporting Sufficiency Test (with worked example)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci span2">
        <div class="ctitle ci">A backstop ensuring adequate overall coverage</div>
        <div class="principle">If TOTAL EXTERNAL REVENUE reported by operating segments is LESS THAN 75% of total consolidated external revenue, ADDITIONAL segments must be added as reportable until 75% is reached.</div>
        <div class="worked worked--flush">
          <div class="wk-title">Example 3: Applying the 75% test</div>
          <div class="wk-body">Total external revenue $50,000,000. Segments meeting 10% thresholds account for $34,000,000 (68%).</div>
          <div class="wk-answer">68% is below 75% — additional segments must be added until at least $37,500,000 (75%) is captured.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="note">Segments not separately reportable are combined in "ALL OTHER SEGMENTS," with revenue sources described.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 7 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s7"><span class="num">7</span><h2>Measurement of Segment Information</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cw span2">
        <div class="ctitle cw">Reported "the way management sees it" — including non-IFRS figures</div>
        <div class="text-supporting">Each segment item is measured as REPORTED TO THE CODM — this can be a NON-IFRS measure, provided the basis and any differences from consolidated totals are EXPLAINED.</div>
        <div class="callout warn callout--flush">⚠️ Segment figures may NOT be directly comparable ACROSS different entities, even in the same industry.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 8 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s8"><span class="num">8</span><h2>Reconciliations to Consolidated Totals</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cn span2">
        <div class="ctitle cn">Bridging segment figures back to the consolidated financial statements</div>
        <div class="text-supporting">Reconciliations required: total segment revenues to entity revenue; total segment profit/loss to entity profit/loss before tax; total segment assets/liabilities to entity totals; and other material items.</div>
        <div class="note">These mandatory reconciliations make the management approach work alongside comparability.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 9 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s9"><span class="num">9</span><h2>Entity-Wide Disclosures</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2">
        <div class="ctitle cd">Required regardless of segment structure — a final backstop</div>
        <div class="two-col">
          <div>
            <div class="elabel">PRODUCTS/SERVICES &amp; GEOGRAPHY</div>
            <div class="check-list">
              <span>Revenue from external customers by product/service, even if segments organised differently</span>
              <span>Revenue attributed to country of domicile vs all foreign countries in total</span>
              <span>Non-current assets located in domicile vs foreign countries in total</span>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div>
            <div class="elabel">MAJOR CUSTOMERS</div>
            <div class="check-list">
              <span>If revenue from a SINGLE customer is 10%+ of total revenue, disclose that fact, the amount, and the segment(s) involved</span>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="callout warn callout--warn-flush">⚠️ These apply EVEN IF the entity has only ONE reportable segment.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 10 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s10"><span class="num">10</span><h2>Critical Judgments &amp; Estimates</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cn span2">
        <div class="ctitle cn">Where management judgement matters most</div>
        <div class="two-col">
          <div>
            <div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT JUDGEMENTS</div>
            <div class="dot-list">
              <span>Identifying the CODM function correctly in complex structures</span>
              <span>Whether operating segments meet the five-factor aggregation criteria</span>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div>
            <div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT ESTIMATES</div>
            <div class="dot-list">
              <span>Allocating shared/corporate costs between segments</span>
              <span>Estimating revenue attributable to a customer's parent for concentration disclosure</span>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="callout warn callout--warn-flush">⚠️ Changes to internal reporting structures can trigger CHANGES in reportable segments year over year.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 11 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s11"><span class="num">11</span><h2>IFRS vs US GAAP</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci span2">
        <div class="ctitle ci">Comparison — IFRS 8 vs US GAAP (ASC 280)</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th cols-12-1-1"><span>Feature</span><span>IFRS (IFRS 8)</span><span>US GAAP</span></div>
          <div class="tr cols-12-1-1"><span>Management approach</span><span>Adopted, modelled on US GAAP</span><span>ASC 280 was the ORIGINAL source</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt cols-12-1-1"><span>10% thresholds &amp; 75% test</span><span>Same mechanics</span><span>Same mechanics, converged</span></div>
        </div>
        <div class="note">IFRS 8 and ASC 280 are among the MOST CONVERGED standards in IFRS/US GAAP.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 12 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s12"><span class="num">12</span><h2>Statement Mapping</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cs span2">
        <div class="ctitle cs">Disclosure-to-location mapping table</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th cols-16-1-14"><span>Disclosure item</span><span>Location</span><span>Notes</span></div>
          <div class="tr cols-16-1-14"><span>Segment revenue, profit/loss, assets, liabilities</span><span><span class="badge bp">Notes</span></span><span>May use non-IFRS internal measures</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt cols-16-1-14"><span>Reconciliation to consolidated totals</span><span><span class="badge bp">Notes</span></span><span>Mandatory bridging disclosure</span></div>
          <div class="tr cols-16-1-14"><span>Entity-wide product/geography disclosures</span><span><span class="badge bp">Notes</span></span><span>Required regardless of segment structure</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt cols-16-1-14"><span>Major customer concentration</span><span><span class="badge bp">Notes</span></span><span>10%+ of total revenue</span></div>
        </div>
        <div class="note">IFRS 8 is entirely a DISCLOSURE standard — no effect on recognised SOFP/SOPL/OCI/Equity figures.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 13 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s13"><span class="num">13</span><h2>Related Standards</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2">
        <div class="ctitle cd">Cross-reference map</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th cols-08-2"><span>Standard</span><span>Connection</span></div>
          <div class="tr cols-08-2"><span>IAS 34</span><span>Requires segment information in interim reports</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt cols-08-2"><span>IFRS 5</span><span>Segments must be identified for held-for-sale/discontinued operations</span></div>
          <div class="tr cols-08-2"><span>IFRS 3</span><span>Goodwill CGUs often align with, though not identical to, operating segments</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt cols-08-2"><span>IFRS 7</span><span>Financial instrument risk disclosures may follow segment structure</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 14 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s14"><span class="num">14</span><h2>Quick-Reference Glossary</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<Glossary entries={[
      { term: "Management approach", definition: "Defining segments based on internal management structure, not an external classification." },
      { term: "All other segments", definition: "Residual category combining non-reportable operating segments." },
      { term: "Intersegment transactions", definition: "Transactions between segments within the same entity, included in the 10% revenue test." },
      { term: "Chief operating decision maker (CODM)", definition: "A function, not a job title, that allocates resources to and assesses performance of operating segments." },
      { term: "Reportable segment", definition: "An operating segment (or aggregation) meeting one of the quantitative thresholds, or voluntarily reported." },
      { term: "75% reporting sufficiency test", definition: "A backstop requiring that at least 75% of total consolidated external revenue be covered by reportable segments; otherwise additional segments must be added." }
    ]} />

<FinancialStatementKey />

  </div>
  <section id="history">
    <HistoryTimeline items={history.IFRS8} />
  </section>
</StandardPageLayout>
